<section class="resource-hero"><div class="container"><div class="resource-hero-grid"><div><span class="eyebrow">Builder guide</span><h1>How to Make a Circle in Minecraft</h1><p>A practical construction method for small and large block circles—without trying to eyeball the curve.</p></div><div class="resource-meta"><span>5 STEPS</span><strong>Center → Segments</strong><small>Practical construction workflow</small></div></div></div></section>
<article class="section guide-article"><div class="container editorial-grid"><aside class="editorial-aside"><div class="section-kicker">Circle build method</div><p>Start from the footprint, establish symmetry, then work row by row.</p><a class="aside-cta" href="/#c-31-thin-1">Open 31×31 blueprint →</a></aside><div class="prose editorial-prose"><h2>1. Choose the outside diameter</h2><p>A 21×21 circle occupies a 21-block-wide square. Odd diameters have one center block; even diameters are centered between the middle four blocks. Decide the footprint before placing your first anchor.</p><h2>2. Mark the center and four anchors</h2><p>From the center, mark the north, south, east and west extremes of the circle. These anchors establish the outside boundary and make symmetry much easier to maintain.</p><h2>3. Build with row segments</h2><p>Do not count individual pixels from an image. Treat every row as one or more horizontal runs. For example, a row might contain two blocks on the left, a gap, and two blocks on the right. VoxelCurve shows the exact columns and center-relative offsets.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Best practice:</strong> complete one quarter or one symmetric row at a time. Mirror it before moving deeper into the curve.</div><h2>4. Check symmetry as you go</h2><p>Every left-side run should have a corresponding right-side run. The same is true for top and bottom halves. If a row looks wrong, fix it immediately instead of carrying the offset into later rows.</p><h2>5. Scale the method for large circles</h2><p>For a 31×31 or larger circle, keep the center marked and use exact segment positions rather than repeatedly recounting from the boundary. This reduces cumulative errors on long walls and arena foundations.</p><h2>Example sizes</h2><h3>11×11</h3><p>A compact size for wells, towers and decorative builds. It is small enough to verify visually after every row.</p><h3>21×21</h3><p>A useful medium footprint for rooms, tower bases and observatories. Marking the center becomes especially valuable.</p><h3>31×31</h3><p>Large enough that a guided row plan saves significant time. Use the generator to keep track of the current segment and material count.</p><h2>Use the interactive blueprint</h2><p><a class="text-link" href="/#c-31-thin-1">Open a 31×31 circle in VoxelCurve →</a></p></div></div></article>
